1
00:00:00,760 --> 00:00:04,050
35 million tokens.
Basically, enough to write

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:09,050
the whole Harry Potter saga more than
20 times, burned in less than a day,

3
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:12,620
with Max Weinbach's keys,
stolen from OpenCode.

4
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:14,930
And yet he didn't run anything.

5
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:19,090
Almost 9,000 requests, his
OpenAI credit at −$2.15

6
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:22,170
and $264 gone on his Anthropic key.

7
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:25,370
And in his logs, a request
in Chinese: "Reply ok."

8
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:26,620
The AI replies…

9
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:31,110
Someone paid, with someone else's
money, to make an AI say "ok".

10
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:32,130
But why?

11
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:36,190
OpenCode's code is public, so
we'll see where these keys were

12
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:39,600
stored and why they couldn't
be hashed like a password.

13
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:42,190
And if you've ever pasted
an API key somewhere,

14
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:45,000
stay until the end, because that's
your checklist for tonight.

15
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:49,630
So, what is OpenCode?

16
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:52,830
OpenCode is an open-source coding
agent that runs in your terminal,

17
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:56,940
a bit like Claude Code or Codex, but
you plug it into any model you want.

18
00:00:57,340 --> 00:00:59,790
Except there are actually two OpenCodes.

19
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:02,620
The desktop app and the Console.

20
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:05,470
The Console is a web
dashboard where you can

21
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:08,640
save your own OpenAI,
Anthropic or Google keys.

22
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:10,980
It's called "bring your own key".

23
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:14,210
Personally, I've never pasted an
API key into a tool like that.

24
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:16,280
I tend to be very careful.

25
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:18,430
So it's the Console that got hit.

26
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:21,910
That same evening, Dax Raad
from OpenCode clarified

27
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:24,180
that it's the Console,
not OpenCode itself.

28
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:26,050
"Those keys never come to us."

29
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:29,210
So basically, if you only
use the app, you can relax.

30
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:30,860
The others... we're not so sure.

31
00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:37,670
The night of October 17, 2
a.m., according to OpenCode,

32
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:40,070
the keys may have been
exposed from that time on.

33
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:42,010
During the day, the bills climb.

34
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:43,770
Max gets an email from Google.

35
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:45,850
His limits have been
"temporarily lowered".

36
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:47,380
Google nerfed his account.

37
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:51,400
5:30 p.m., Matt Johansen posts
the email customers received.

38
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:53,720
"Confirmed. OpenCode hacked. Keys stolen."

39
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:56,810
8 p.m., OpenCode confirms a "large scale

40
00:01:56,860 --> 00:02:01,160
distillation attack", with 1.4%
of Console users affected.

41
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:03,140
Remember that word: distillation.

42
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:05,750
Anthropic and Google block the keys.

43
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:08,480
OpenAI refunds, according to TechCrunch.

44
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:10,890
Also according to TechCrunch,
while analyzing the attack,

45
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:13,910
Dax found it "a bit frustrating"
that the AIs refused to help him.

46
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:17,630
The guardrails meant to block hackers
were actually blocking the victim.

47
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:20,650
But how do keys end up out in the wild?

48
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:21,840
Let's open the code.

49
00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:28,870
The Console's code is on
GitHub, with its full history.

50
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:31,370
Until October 12, there's
a "provider" table

51
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:34,610
with a "credentials" column, of type text.

52
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:37,010
When you save your key, it lands

53
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:39,520
in that column, exactly as you pasted it.

54
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:41,650
The proof is the settings page.

55
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:44,750
It shows your key masked:
the first 8 characters,

56
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:47,230
3 dots and the last 8 characters.

57
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:50,530
And that mask is computed
from the stored value.

58
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:53,840
To show you the end of your
key, they need your key.

59
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:57,170
So it's fair to ask: why

60
00:02:57,220 --> 00:02:58,930
didn't they hash it like a password?

61
00:02:58,980 --> 00:03:00,780
Take two seconds to think about it.

62
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:05,490
A password, if the site
never needs to read it back,

63
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:07,680
it hashes what you type

64
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:09,450
and compares the two fingerprints.

65
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,570
An API key has to be sent in full
to the provider with every request.

66
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:14,470
A fingerprint is useless to OpenAI.

67
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:18,250
The only one who can make do with a
fingerprint is whoever makes the key.

68
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:20,030
They just check the one you show them.

69
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:23,660
A tool that uses it on your behalf
has to be able to read it back.

70
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:25,930
So an API key gets encrypted, with a

71
00:03:25,980 --> 00:03:27,930
second key stored somewhere
else, in a vault.

72
00:03:27,980 --> 00:03:31,310
To read it, the hacker has to
steal two things instead of one.

73
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:33,580
In this code, there's no vault.

74
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:36,590
To be fair, the host
may encrypt its disks.

75
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:39,310
That protects you if someone
walks off with the disk,

76
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:42,320
not against someone who can
query the database directly.

77
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:45,830
If you like seeing this kind of thing
under the hood of the tools you

78
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:48,870
use every day, subscribe
to my channel, Technovore.

79
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:52,640
We open them up one by one, and it's the only
subscription that will never charge you anything.

80
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:59,150
In the email sent to victims,
OpenCode mentions access

81
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:02,180
to a "legacy system that we were in
the process of decommissioning".

82
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:04,560
Nothing links what follows to that system.

83
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:06,770
OpenCode hasn't said
where the hacker got in.

84
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:09,570
But honestly, it's a trap
every dev should know about.

85
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:14,000
On October 12, a commit removes the
old database from the Console's code.

86
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:17,790
In their infrastructure
code, this production

87
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:20,190
database was never created by the code.

88
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:21,650
It's only referenced.

89
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:23,370
Basically: it exists.

90
00:04:23,420 --> 00:04:24,420
It's there.

91
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:28,350
Its password, created by the code,
is deleted on the next deploy.

92
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:29,600
The database stays.

93
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:33,370
Basically, it's like removing a
character from your party in a game.

94
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:35,130
They disappear from your screen,
but they're still waiting

95
00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:37,680
at the tavern with their whole inventory.

96
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:42,470
I still have my question from the start.

97
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:45,080
Why pay, with someone
else's money, for an "ok"?

98
00:04:45,260 --> 00:04:48,740
To answer that, you need to know
what an API key is worth in 2026.

99
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:52,570
The word in the announcement, distillation,
means copying off the top student.

100
00:04:52,620 --> 00:04:55,530
You ask a big model millions
of questions and you train

101
00:04:55,580 --> 00:04:59,010
a small model on the answers,
for much less money.

102
00:04:59,060 --> 00:05:01,930
In February 2026, Anthropic
accused DeepSeek,

103
00:05:01,980 --> 00:05:06,170
Moonshot and MiniMax of doing this
with about 24,000 fake accounts.

104
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:08,830
On October 12, they
announce new defenses, with

105
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:11,940
fake accounts often banned
in under two hours.

106
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:16,360
And one of those protections only
covers accounts created from August 31.

107
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:18,670
This part is a hypothesis,
nobody has confirmed it.

108
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:22,350
For a hacker, a real developer's
account opened before

109
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:25,680
that date is probably worth much
more than a brand-new fake account.

110
00:05:25,900 --> 00:05:27,790
And stolen keys get resold.

111
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:31,410
In its September report, Anthropic
writes that stealing API keys

112
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:34,450
has become the sole objective
of several criminal groups.

113
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:38,200
Resale networks rotate stolen
keys until they're drained.

114
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:41,460
It's a bit like farming,
but with your credit card.

115
00:05:41,840 --> 00:05:45,490
In July, with no link to
the leak, Dax said he had

116
00:05:45,540 --> 00:05:49,440
banned a single person who ran
8,000 fake OpenCode accounts.

117
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:52,190
$480,000 of tokens resold every month.

118
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:53,440
Come on, that's too much.

119
00:05:53,580 --> 00:05:57,900
In the end, an API key is a credit
card already plugged into an AI.

120
00:05:58,320 --> 00:05:59,310
So, that "ok"?

121
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:01,110
Max posted it as an example.

122
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:02,870
One request out of almost 9,000.

123
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:04,520
Not necessarily the first one.

124
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:06,010
On its own, it proves nothing.

125
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:08,550
But you don't train a
model with "reply ok".

126
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:09,890
It looks more like a test.

127
00:06:09,940 --> 00:06:11,590
Does this key work?

128
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:14,770
Filing a key theft under
the word distillation:

129
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:17,190
Nathan Lambert finds that very odd.

130
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:19,770
And a request in Chinese
doesn't tell you who sent it.

131
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:23,050
To date, OpenCode has published neither
the entry point nor the number of keys.

132
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:24,940
We don't know who, or why.

133
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:30,130
Now, your own key.

134
00:06:30,180 --> 00:06:34,480
The day it leaks, how much you lose
depends mostly on one setting: your cap.

135
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:37,170
Look at Max's −$2.15.

136
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:38,830
His credit went below zero.

137
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:41,400
The meter doesn't stop exactly at zero.

138
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:44,350
At OpenAI, a spend alert blocks nothing.

139
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:45,780
It's in their docs.

140
00:06:45,940 --> 00:06:47,990
So you need a real limit, separately.

141
00:06:48,040 --> 00:06:50,720
And even that limit isn't
enforced instantly.

142
00:06:50,940 --> 00:06:52,630
At Anthropic, it's prepaid.

143
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:54,260
When it's empty, it stops.

144
00:06:54,760 --> 00:06:57,120
Unless you turned on auto-reload.

145
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:00,400
Then your balance tops up with
your card, for the hacker.

146
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:03,080
Honestly, the worst
subscription of your life.

147
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:07,850
With no setting, your
cap is your tier's cap.

148
00:07:07,900 --> 00:07:12,000
$500 a month on the first tier,
$200,000 at the very top.

149
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:14,310
At Google, the Gemini bill can lag

150
00:07:14,360 --> 00:07:16,630
by up to about ten minutes.

151
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:18,980
For a script running flat
out, that's a long time.

152
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:21,890
So, tonight:

153
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:25,890
1. If you got the email, revoke your
key at the provider right away.

154
00:07:25,940 --> 00:07:28,320
Deleting it from the
Console doesn't disable it.

155
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:31,510
2. No email, but a Claude key in OpenCode?

156
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:34,140
Anthropic recommends creating
a new one, to be safe.

157
00:07:34,340 --> 00:07:35,770
3. One key per tool.

158
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:36,910
If it leaks, you know where from.

159
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:38,540
And you only cut that one.

160
00:07:39,020 --> 00:07:42,060
4. A real limit, and no auto-reload.

161
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:43,850
5. Delete forgotten keys.

162
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:46,520
We all have some lying
around from an old tutorial.

163
00:07:46,820 --> 00:07:48,370
As for me, I told you from the start,

164
00:07:48,420 --> 00:07:51,290
I've never pasted a key
into this kind of tool.

165
00:07:51,340 --> 00:07:53,230
That said, I do plan to use OpenCode.

166
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:55,990
And after reading this code, I'm not about

167
00:07:56,040 --> 00:07:58,580
to start pasting API keys everywhere.

168
00:07:58,860 --> 00:08:04,030
On my RTX 5080, I can run
local models with no API key.

169
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:05,750
The only bill is electricity.

170
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:08,810
Can that really replace
a Claude subscription?

171
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:12,790
In the meantime, if you want to see
Claude in good hands, it's right here:

172
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:15,690
a single developer
recreated Adobe's 7 apps.

173
00:08:15,740 --> 00:08:20,660
And the only key nobody can steal
from you is the one you don't have.
